{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_uhhxkxitckeiz27sbffoylr2jf3ia3t5xzoqlmzflnbjhlrwkfoq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Result\n\nThe Rust translation of `encode_quant` is **byte-identical to the C** across every input class tested. No divergences were found — the translation was already correct; the real defects were in my test harness, which I found and fixed.\n\n**Phase A** — artifacts in `translation/`: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (27 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (35 rows).\n\n**Phase B/C** — 61 differential tests (33 valid-path + 28 error-path), all passing. Both libraries are loaded via `libloading` and called through their exported `encode_quant` symbol, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrapper is exercised too. Coverage includes ~1M randomized calls (fixed seed), an exhaustive `uni ∈ -64..=64` × `lsbit ∈ -4..=9` grid, and the full 6⁶ extreme-value cross-product.\n\n**Phase D** — symbol diff is empty (`encode_quant` is the only export). Cargo.toml has no `[features]` table, so `{default, --no-default-features}` is the complete configuration set; both pass, in both release and debug (debug enables overflow checks, confirming the `wrapping_*` usage is right). No binary target exists in either build, so there is no stdout to compare.\n\n### Two harness bugs worth flagging\n\n1. **`cargo test` does not rebuild a `cdylib`.** My first green run was against a stale `.so` — a deliberately mutated Rust source (`d2 < d0` → `d2 <= d0`) still passed all 61 tests. I added `assert_fresh` (fails if either `.so` is older than its sources) and `run_tests.sh` (rebuilds both first). Re-running the mutation now trips 19+ rows. I also self-tested the guard: touching `src/lib.rs` without rebuilding fails every test with `STALE SHARED OBJECT`.\n\n2. **Three coverage assertions targeted conditions random search can't reach.** Investigating rather than relaxing them produced two findings about the C:\n   - `diff == INT_MIN` (ERRORS row 6) is **unreachable** — `(m*step)/8` confines `diff` to `[-2²⁸, 2²⁸-1]`, so the `diff = -diff` on line 32 can never overflow. The test now proves that bound and reaches the *attainable* extremes by solving `step = product · m⁻¹ mod 2³²` (the multiplier `2*(uni&7)+1` is always odd, hence invertible), since that window is 8 wide out of 2³².\n   - The \"both candidates better\" case (row 25) is unreachable for non-overflowing inputs — `d(p)` is convex and `p0` lies between `p1` and `p2`, so `d0 ≤ max(d1,d2)`. It only occurs once the subtractions overflow, so the test now draws full-range. When it does occur, the C's second `if` overwrites the first and returns `uni2` even when `uni1` was strictly better; the Rust reproduces this quirk.\n\nOne caveat on scope: this library is a single 62-line pure function, so \"all inputs\" is 6 × 32 bits — far too large to exhaust. The evidence is strong (every branch and boundary is covered, and the suite is mutation-sensitive) but not a proof of total equivalence."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6643,"cache_read_input_tokens":125128,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6643,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"70e28745-9584-46db-9f42-c57a7e624ccc","uuid":"14613b29-8032-42fa-8a7a-f538581167f0","timestamp":"2026-09-05T00:21:34.312Z"}
